  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1227 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nSeasonably Warm & Windy Through Wednesday:\n\nA tightening low level pressure gradient within return flow will \nincrease south-southwest winds today and Wednesday, strongest in \nsusceptible, open areas west of the Mississippi River Valley. LREF \nprobabilities for 20 kt gusts push 100% during this time as \nprobabilities for 34 kts remain limited across the area. Anomalous \nlow level warmth, highest (+15C at 850mb) over southern Canada at \nforecast issuance, remain off to the north of the forecast area \nuntil Thursday, persisting 850mb temperatures 5-10C above normal for \nthe time of year compared to model climatology. High surface \ntemperatures in the mid 50s until Thursday with some pushing the low \n60s. \n\nWeekend Precipitation Potential:\n\nPrevious forecast precipitation chances for Wednesday night into \nThursday are now below mentionable (<10%), in line with local LREF \nmember probability trends for measurable precipitation. Less inter-\nensemble agreement for subsequent precipitation chances locally this \nweekend as most recent EPS(16.18Z)/GEFS(17.00Z) decreased/increased \nlocal probabilities for 0.1\" of rainfall in 24 hours compared to \ntheir respective previous runs. \n\nVariability in solutions is tied to advection and accompanying \nstrength of surface low shifting northeast, near/northwest of the \nlocal forecast area through the weekend. The higher precipitation \npotential solution (GFS/GEFS) suggests cyclogenesis along a \nlingering baroclinic boundary as upstream mid level positive \nvorticity advection persists through the Upper Midwest while the \nlower precipitation probability solution (EPS/ECMWF) quickly advects \nthe associated surface low north/northeast of the forecast area with \na quicker, deeper upper level trough causing a more transient \nsynoptic pattern and weakening mid level vorticity advection. \nRegardless, increased consensus on timing of frontal boundary \npassage resulted in an increase of NBM PoPs near 70% through \nSaturday, slightly later than previous forecasts. Overall impacts \nexpected to be minimal with very limited, if any instability and \nprobabilities for amounts 0.1\" to 0.25\". Should a slower solution \ncontinue to be suggested, permitting additional moisture, perhaps \nimpacts may increase somewhat although not overly concerning at the \ncurrent forecast hour other than increased winds through the \nweekend. Current GEFS (17.00Z) confidence for 34+ kt gusts nears 10% \nwhile EPS remains at 0%.\n\nCooler, More Seasonable Temperatures From Saturday Night:\n\nSimilar to previous forecasts, initial onset of precipitation \nremains variable in forecast guidance even though overall \nimpacts appears minimal. Below freezing temperatures behind the\nfrontal boundary will return more seasonable conditions to the \nlocal forecast area, after precipitation chances cease through \nlater in the week.",
